Sometimes I wonder if I should have chosen engineering over design. The allure is real. My analytical bent of mind often draws me towards understanding how things work, how systems interact and how seemingly unrelated variables influence one another.

But I also love noticing deeper patterns in human behaviour, history, politics and culture. The things that, eventually, govern the decisions that run the world.

Perhaps that's why I've been thinking about a rather old fable.

## 1. The golden goose fumble

!["I am tired of this world-these people. I am tired of being caught in the tangle of their lives." "They claim their labors are to build a heaven, yet their heaven is populated with horrors."](@assets/images/posts/the-golden-goose-fumble/image.png)

The story of the golden goose is deeply embedded in the human psyche. It serves as a cautionary tale: the pursuit of more, driven by greed and impatience, can eventually destroy the very source of abundance.

In its material interpretation, a golden goose might represent a million-dollar idea, a successful business or a coveted title. Except this time, we've failed to recognise our own reflection in the goose's eyes, let alone protect it.

Consider the declining fertility rates across the world, particularly in developed economies. The global fertility rate stood at approximately 2.2 births per woman in 2024, with more than half of all countries and territories already below the replacement level of 2.1{% footnote n="1" /%} Across OECD countries, the average has fallen further, to around 1.46.{% footnote n="2" /%}

These numbers represent more than a demographic challenge. They raise a rather uncomfortable question about the future we are collectively building.

For decades, we've optimised for productivity, economic growth, consumption and individual convenience. Yet the conditions required to sustain the next generation, affordable housing, economic security, stable employment and the time to raise a family, have become increasingly difficult to secure. Even the OECD recognises these everyday constraints as important factors influencing fertility decisions.{% footnote n="2" ref="b" /%}

The golden goose, in this interpretation, is humanity's capacity to sustain its own future.

And the troubling part is that there is no quick fix. No technological breakthrough, government incentive or AI-powered intervention can independently solve a problem rooted in the way we've organised society.

If anything, the competitive incentives surrounding AI risk aggravating some of these pressures. As explored in *The AI Layoff Trap* by Brett Hemenway Falk and Gerry Tsoukalas, individual companies can be incentivised to automate beyond what is collectively beneficial. Their research demonstrates how competing firms can end up in an automation arms race, displacing workers beyond what would be economically optimal for the collective.{% footnote n="3" /%}

We may be getting exceptionally good at extracting value from the present while undermining the conditions that make a future possible.

## 2. The narrative disconnect; Dr. Manhattan

There's another, perhaps more immediate, consequence of this way of thinking.

Whenever people are repeatedly told that they are replaceable, a culture of dehumanisation begins to take shape. The value of labour, experience and individual contribution gets reduced to a metric. A person's struggles, ambitions and accomplishments become secondary to their economic utility.

And when pushed too far, people either retreat into nihilism or revolt.

Jim Prosser, in his essay *AI, "Humanity", and Dr. Manhattan Syndrome*, uses the character of Dr. Manhattan from the graphic novel *Watchmen* as a metaphor for this phenomenon.{% footnote n="4" /%}

Dr. Manhattan is a godlike being who perceives the world through an almost incomprehensible understanding of quantum physics and time. He can comprehend humanity's existence on a cosmic scale, but gradually loses his ability to empathise with individual human beings.

He cares about humanity as an abstract concept, but struggles to care about actual humans.

Prosser draws a parallel with corporate leadership and the people building increasingly powerful technologies. There's an unsettling tendency to celebrate Humanity as an abstract beneficiary of technological progress while treating actual people, with their grievances, livelihoods and anxieties, as inconvenient variables in the equation.

The distinction matters.

It's entirely possible to optimise a system for humanity while making life worse for the humans living inside it.

Perhaps the problem isn't that we've become too analytical. It's that we've become comfortable defining success without adequately accounting for the people who are supposed to benefit from it.

## 3. The way ahead; what our past indicates

![](@assets/images/posts/the-golden-goose-fumble/image.png)

The outcome isn't predetermined. And perhaps that's the one thing working in our favour.

Humans are remarkably resilient. More importantly, we possess something that doesn't always lend itself to rational explanation: hope. The ability to create meaning, cultivate virtue and find purpose even in the face of adversity and inherent uncertainty.

History offers some interesting precedents.

The concentration of corporate power and wealth during America's Gilded Age eventually fuelled movements that helped shape the Progressive Era. Organised labour, investigative journalism and political reform challenged the excesses of industrial capitalism, contributing to stronger worker protections, antitrust measures and consumer rights.{% footnote n="5" /%}

Similarly, the disillusionment that followed the First World War became part of the cultural backdrop against which an extraordinary period of artistic experimentation and social change unfolded during the 1920s.

Of course, history doesn't move in neat circles. Hardship doesn't automatically produce progress. It can just as easily produce apathy, authoritarianism and further fragmentation.

But it can also create the conditions for people to question the systems they've inherited.

Today, Gen Z finds itself navigating a peculiar contradiction. It is perhaps the most digitally connected generation in history, yet increasingly confronted with algorithmic isolation, wealth inequality, economic uncertainty and the prospect of rapid technological displacement.

There are early signs of a countercurrent. The growing visibility of community-focused volunteering, independent media and more optimistic cultural expressions suggests that not everyone is willing to accept digital isolation and economic productivity as the defining features of modern life.

Perhaps we're beginning to rediscover the value of things that don't scale particularly well: friendships, communities, art, belonging and shared experiences.

And maybe that's where the answer lies.

Saving the golden goose hence, is recognising that progress has very little meaning if it gradually erodes the human conditions that make progress worthwhile.

Whether we're able to save the golden goose might ultimately depend on how much we're willing to believe in a future beyond ourselves.

