## Appendix

### Tragedy of the Commons

Consider a shared database serving multiple microservices. Each service independently queries the database whenever it needs information. From the perspective of an individual service, making more requests is perfectly rational: it gets the data it needs, when it needs it.

But when every service behaves this way without accounting for the collective load, the database becomes overwhelmed and eventually collapses.

What is individually rational becomes collectively destructive.

This is an analogy for the tragedy of the commons: a shared resource is depleted when individuals optimise for their own interests without accounting for the consequences imposed on everyone else.

The problem doesn't solve itself. It requires coordination, shared responsibility or mechanisms that change the incentives.

### Dominant Strategy

A dominant strategy is one that produces the best outcome for an individual player, regardless of what the other players choose.

A prisoner's dilemma is a particular game-theoretic situation in which individually rational decisions lead to a collectively worse outcome.

In competitive markets, this creates an interesting parallel. Every company may have an incentive to automate, cut costs and move faster than its competitors, even when the collective outcome is detrimental to everyone.

The AI automation race is an interesting example. The incentives of individual companies can push the entire industry towards an outcome that none of them would necessarily choose if they could coordinate.

